<h2>Start with primary evidence</h2><p>We prioritise original publications and datasets from FAO, WFP, IPC, WHO, national statistical offices, agriculture ministries and other relevant public bodies. Secondary reporting can alert us to a development, but key numbers should be traced to the source where feasible. Where no primary source is available, the limitation should be stated.</p>
<h2>Every number needs context</h2><p>Important statistics should include the publishing organisation, reference period, geography, units and definition. For forecasts, we state the projection period and avoid writing as if the projected result has already occurred. A global commodity-price index is not a supermarket price index; national production is not proof that every household can afford food.</p>
<h2>Separate claim types</h2><ul><li><strong>Observed:</strong> measured or reported for a stated period.</li><li><strong>Estimated:</strong> calculated from available evidence and subject to revision.</li><li><strong>Forecast or projection:</strong> a possible future result under stated assumptions.</li><li><strong>Early warning:</strong> an alert about a risk that may worsen; not necessarily confirmation that the worst outcome occurred.</li><li><strong>Interpretation:</strong> our explanation of what the evidence may mean, with caveats noted.</li></ul>
<h2>Country coverage and denominator changes</h2><p>Country-level reports may cover selected provinces, districts, assessed populations, control areas or humanitarian-access zones rather than every resident. When assessment coverage changes between releases, totals can change even without an equivalent improvement or deterioration. We aim to flag those differences and link to the full report.</p>
<h2>Article updates and corrections</h2><p>Time-sensitive articles should carry a visible updated or reviewed date and distinguish it from dates attached to the source data. When important evidence changes, the text and structured metadata should be reviewed. Readers can report an issue from the <a href="/contact/">corrections page</a>; the correction should be specific and proportionate.</p>
<h2>Authorship and independence</h2><p>Articles use the <strong>World Food Crisis Editorial Desk</strong> byline. This is a publisher byline for this site, not a claim that a named subject expert or an external agency reviewed the article. Source organisations are cited for the data and analysis they publish; citations do not imply endorsement of our conclusions.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2><p>We publish educational explainers, not real-time emergency alerts or personal advice. In fast-changing situations, use the linked primary report and check whether a newer release has superseded the figures quoted here.</p></div></main><footer class="site-footer"><div class="container footer-main"><div class="footer-brand"><a class="brand brand-footer" href="/"><span class="brand-mark">F<span>+</span></span><span class="brand-words"><strong>World Food Crisis</strong><small>Facts before fear.</small></span></a><p>Independent explainers on food availability, prices and food security.</p></div><div class="footer-col"><strong>Explore</strong><a href="/data/">Data desk</a><a href="/sources/">Sources</a><a href="/methodology/">Methodology</a></div><div class="footer-col"><strong>About</strong><a href="/about/">Our purpose</a><a href="/contact/">Corrections & contact</a><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a></div></div><div class="container footer-bottom"><span>© <span data-year>2026</span> World Food Crisis</span><span>Sources over speculation.</span></div></footer></body></html>